#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""foxwar - Foxhole persistent-war status in the terminal.

Data: the official Foxhole War API (war state, per-region casualties), the
Steam concurrent-player count, and foxholestats.com's Live Event Log (scraped -
it has 72h of history and no official equivalent). Renders casualties, the
victory-town race, per-region control, an ASCII front-line map with invasion
markers, and the capture log.

Usage:
  foxwar                 summary: victory-town race, per-side casualties and
                         rate, players, invasion alerts, last 8 events
  foxwar map             ASCII hex map; regions with an invasion alert are flagged
  foxwar regions         table: control, per-side casualties and rate
  foxwar events [N]      last N base/structure events (default 30)
  foxwar watch [SECS]    live: map + most-active regions + events + summary (60s)
  foxwar poll [SECS]     headless: refresh casualty tracking, print new events
  foxwar tmux            one compact line for a tmux status bar

Options:
  --shard N              live shard 1, 2 or 3            (env FOXWAR_SHARD)
  --no-color             plain output
  --blink                blink the invasion-alert markers

A region is 'contested' (⚔) when both factions hold a base in it (town, relic
or keep). A real Foxhole invasion (an enemy-held border base pushing into a hex
you still fully own, ending when they take a base) isn't in any API; foxwar
raises an INVASION ALERT (‼) when foxholestats logged a base captured/started
there in the last 45 min (FOXWAR_INVASION_WINDOW) or a fully-owned front-line
hex's casualty rate is clearly elevated (FOXWAR_HOT_RATE etc).

The event log comes straight from foxholestats so it's populated on first run.
Casualty rates need ~2 min of local history and sharpen over ~30 min; keep
`foxwar poll` (the foxwar-poll systemd service) or the tmux bar running to
build it. Everything is cached under ~/.cache/foxwar.
"""
